::MCR_U05_L06_GuidedPractice_Q01::
Question ID: MCR_U05_L06_GP_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.PR.8
Question:
A game has a 1/2 chance to win $6 and a 1/2 chance to win $0. What is the expected value?
{
=A. $3#Correct. (1/2)($6) + (1/2)($0) \= $3.
~B. $6#This assumes the win happens every time. Expected value weights by probability.
~C. $0#This ignores the chance to win $6.
~D. $12#This adds without using probability.
}
::MCR_U05_L06_GuidedPractice_Q02::
Question ID: MCR_U05_L06_GP_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.PR.8
Question:
A choice has a 1/4 chance to gain $20 and a 3/4 chance to gain $0. What is the expected value?
{
~A. $20#This assumes the gain happens every time.
=B. $5#Correct. (1/4)($20) + (3/4)($0) \= $5.
~C. $15#This incorrectly uses the losing probability with the winning amount.
~D. $0#This ignores the chance to gain $20.
}
::MCR_U05_L06_GuidedPractice_Q03::
Question ID: MCR_U05_L06_GP_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.PR.9
Question:
A game costs $3. It pays $9 with probability 1/3 and $0 with probability 2/3. What is the expected net value?
{
~A. $3#This uses the prize average without subtracting the cost correctly.
~B. $9#This assumes the player wins every time.
=C. $0#Correct. Net win is $6 and net loss is -$3, so (1/3)($6) + (2/3)(-$3) \= $0.
~D. -$3#This assumes the player always loses the cost.
}
::MCR_U05_L06_GuidedPractice_Q04::
Question ID: MCR_U05_L06_GP_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.PR.9
Question:
What does an expected value of $0 mean for a game?
{
~A. The player wins every time#A fair expected value does not guarantee a win.
~B. The player loses every time#A fair expected value does not guarantee a loss.
~C. The game has no outcomes#The game can have outcomes even when expected value is 0.
=D. The game is fair on average#Correct. Expected value of 0 means no long-run advantage for either side.
}
::MCR_U05_L06_GuidedPractice_Q05::
Question ID: MCR_U05_L06_GP_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.PR.8
Question:
A decision has a 1/2 chance to gain $10 and a 1/2 chance to lose $4. What is the expected value?
{
~A. $10#This ignores the chance of losing money.
=B. $3#Correct. (1/2)($10) + (1/2)(-$4) \= $5 - $2 \= $3.
~C. -$4#This ignores the chance of gaining money.
~D. $14#This adds the absolute values instead of using signs and probability.
}
